1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:02,933
I’ll go look after the stock.

2
00:00:03,233 --> 00:00:04,433
Quick, Dorothy!

3
00:00:04,733 --> 00:00:05,999
Run for the cellar!

4
00:00:06,299 --> 00:00:10,832
You are welcome, most noble Sorceress, to the land of the Munchkins.

5
00:00:11,132 --> 00:00:15,998
We are so grateful to you for having killed the Wicked Witch of the East,

6
00:00:16,298 --> 00:00:19,298
and for setting our people free from bondage.

7
00:00:19,598 --> 00:00:22,931
You are very kind, but there must be some mistake.

8
00:00:23,231 --> 00:00:25,031
I have not killed anything.

9
00:00:25,331 --> 00:00:27,131
and that is the same thing.

10
00:00:27,431 --> 00:00:32,097
There are her two feet, still sticking out from under a block of wood.

11
00:00:32,397 --> 00:00:34,663
The house must have fallen on her.

12
00:00:34,963 --> 00:00:36,363
Whatever shall we do?

13
00:00:36,663 --> 00:00:37,863
But who was she?

14
00:00:38,163 --> 00:00:41,963
She has held all the Munchkins in bondage for many years,

15
00:00:42,263 --> 00:00:44,929
making them slave for her night and day.

16
00:00:45,229 --> 00:00:49,562
Now they are all set free, and are grateful to you for the favor.

17
00:00:49,862 --> 00:00:51,328
Who are the Munchkins?

18
00:00:51,628 --> 00:00:57,161
They are the people who live in this land of the East where the Wicked Witch ruled.

19
00:00:57,461 --> 00:00:58,727
Are you a Munchkin?

20
00:00:59,027 --> 00:01:03,560
No, but I am their friend, although I live in the land of the North.

21
00:01:03,860 --> 00:01:09,726
When they saw the Witch of the East was dead the Munchkins sent a swift messenger to me,

22
00:01:10,026 --> 00:01:11,292
and I came at once.

23
00:01:11,592 --> 00:01:13,458
I am the Witch of the North.

24
00:01:13,758 --> 00:01:14,958
Oh, gracious!

25
00:01:15,258 --> 00:01:16,658
Are you a real witch?

26
00:01:16,958 --> 00:01:20,024
But I am a good witch, and the people love me.

27
00:01:20,324 --> 00:01:24,324
I am not as powerful as the Wicked Witch was who ruled here,

28
00:01:24,624 --> 00:01:27,557
or I should have set the people free myself.

29
00:01:27,857 --> 00:01:32,590
Aunt Em has told me that the witches were all dead—years and years ago.

30
00:01:32,890 --> 00:01:34,090
Who is Aunt Em?

31
00:01:34,390 --> 00:01:37,990
She is my aunt who lives in Kansas, where I came from.

32
00:01:38,290 --> 00:01:43,890
I do not know where Kansas is, for I have never heard that country mentioned before.

33
00:01:44,190 --> 00:01:46,790
But tell me, is it a civilized country?

34
00:01:47,090 --> 00:01:48,823
Then that accounts for it.

35
00:01:49,123 --> 00:01:54,189
In the civilized countries I believe there are no witches left, nor wizards,

36
00:01:54,489 --> 00:01:56,555
nor sorceresses, nor magicians.

37
00:01:56,855 --> 00:02:00,455
But, you see, the Land of Oz has never been civilized,

38
00:02:00,755 --> 00:02:04,088
for we are cut off from all the rest of the world.

39
00:02:04,388 --> 00:02:08,054
Therefore we still have witches and wizards amongst us.

40
00:02:08,354 --> 00:02:09,687
Who are the wizards?

41
00:02:09,987 --> 00:02:13,520
He is more powerful than all the rest of us together.

42
00:02:13,820 --> 00:02:16,020
He lives in the City of Emeralds.

43
00:02:16,320 --> 00:02:17,520
What is it?

44
00:02:17,820 --> 00:02:20,286
that she dried up quickly in the sun.

45
00:02:20,586 --> 00:02:22,119
That is the end of her.

46
00:02:22,419 --> 00:02:26,685
But the silver shoes are yours, and you shall have them to wear.

47
00:02:26,985 --> 00:02:31,918
and there is some charm connected with them; but what it is we never knew.

48
00:02:32,218 --> 00:02:35,284
I am anxious to get back to my aunt and uncle,

49
00:02:35,584 --> 00:02:38,184
for I am sure they will worry about me.

50
00:02:38,484 --> 00:02:40,350
Can you help me find my way?

51
00:02:40,650 --> 00:02:44,450
there is a great desert, and none could live to cross it.

52
00:02:44,750 --> 00:02:47,016
for I have been there and seen it.

53
00:02:47,316 --> 00:02:50,116
The South is the country of the Quadlings.

54
00:02:50,416 --> 00:02:52,549
that it is the same at the West.

55
00:02:52,849 --> 00:02:58,382
And that country, where the Winkies live, is ruled by the Wicked Witch of the West,

56
00:02:58,682 --> 00:03:02,082
who would make you her slave if you passed her way.

57
00:03:02,382 --> 00:03:07,182
and at its edge is the same great desert that surrounds this Land of Oz.

58
00:03:07,482 --> 00:03:10,882
I’m afraid, my dear, you will have to live with us.

59
00:03:11,182 --> 00:03:13,182
Is your name Dorothy, my dear?

60
00:03:13,482 --> 00:03:16,215
Then you must go to the City of Emeralds.

61
00:03:16,515 --> 00:03:18,181
Perhaps Oz will help you.

62
00:03:18,481 --> 00:03:19,747
Where is this city?

63
00:03:20,047 --> 00:03:24,247
It is exactly in the center of the country, and is ruled by Oz,

64
00:03:24,547 --> 00:03:26,613
the Great Wizard I told you of.

65
00:03:26,913 --> 00:03:28,113
Is he a good man?

66
00:03:28,413 --> 00:03:29,746
He is a good Wizard.

67
00:03:30,046 --> 00:03:34,579
Whether he is a man or not I cannot tell, for I have never seen him.

68
00:03:34,879 --> 00:03:36,212
How can I get there?

69
00:03:36,512 --> 00:03:37,712
You must walk.

70
00:03:38,012 --> 00:03:39,412
It is a long journey,

71
00:03:39,712 --> 00:03:44,845
through a country that is sometimes pleasant and sometimes dark and terrible.

72
00:03:45,145 --> 00:03:49,878
However, I will use all the magic arts I know of to keep you from harm.

73
00:03:50,178 --> 00:03:51,578
Won’t you go with me?

74
00:03:51,878 --> 00:03:53,744
but I will give you my kiss,

75
00:03:54,044 --> 00:03:59,577
and no one will dare injure a person who has been kissed by the Witch of the North.

76
00:03:59,877 --> 00:04:01,343
so you cannot miss it.

77
00:04:01,643 --> 00:04:04,509
When you get to Oz do not be afraid of him,

78
00:04:04,809 --> 00:04:07,742
but tell your story and ask him to help you.

79
00:04:08,042 --> 00:04:09,242
Good-bye, my dear.

80
00:04:09,542 --> 00:04:14,942
They would be just the thing to take a long walk in, for they could not wear out.

81
00:04:15,242 --> 00:04:20,842
We will go to the Emerald City and ask the Great Oz how to get back to Kansas again.

82
00:04:21,142 --> 00:04:23,142
You must be a great sorceress.

83
00:04:23,442 --> 00:04:27,642
Because you wear silver shoes and have killed the Wicked Witch.

84
00:04:27,942 --> 00:04:33,475
Besides, you have white in your frock, and only witches and sorceresses wear white.

85
00:04:33,775 --> 00:04:38,108
Blue is the color of the Munchkins, and white is the witch color.

86
00:04:38,408 --> 00:04:40,808
So we know you are a friendly witch.

87
00:04:41,108 --> 00:04:43,374
How far is it to the Emerald City?

88
00:04:43,674 --> 00:04:45,540
for I have never been there.

89
00:04:45,840 --> 00:04:51,240
It is better for people to keep away from Oz, unless they have business with him.

90
00:04:51,540 --> 00:04:56,406
But it is a long way to the Emerald City, and it will take you many days.

91
00:04:56,706 --> 00:04:59,239
The country here is rich and pleasant,

92
00:04:59,539 --> 00:05:05,805
but you must pass through rough and dangerous places before you reach the end of your journey.

93
00:05:06,105 --> 00:05:07,305
Did you speak?

94
00:05:07,605 --> 00:05:08,805
How do you do?

95
00:05:09,105 --> 00:05:10,305
How do you do?

96
00:05:10,605 --> 00:05:15,871
for it is very tedious being perched up here night and day to scare away crows.

97
00:05:16,171 --> 00:05:17,437
Can’t you get down?

98
00:05:17,737 --> 00:05:20,270
No, for this pole is stuck up my back.

99
00:05:20,570 --> 00:05:25,370
If you will please take away the pole I shall be greatly obliged to you.

100
00:05:25,670 --> 00:05:27,136
I feel like a new man.

101
00:05:27,436 --> 00:05:28,636
Who are you?

102
00:05:28,936 --> 00:05:30,536
And where are you going?

103
00:05:30,836 --> 00:05:36,302
and I am going to the Emerald City, to ask the Great Oz to send me back to Kansas.

104
00:05:36,602 --> 00:05:38,335
Where is the Emerald City?

105
00:05:38,635 --> 00:05:39,835
And who is Oz?

106
00:05:40,135 --> 00:05:41,468
Why, don’t you know?

107
00:05:41,768 --> 00:05:42,968
No, indeed.

108
00:05:43,268 --> 00:05:44,734
I don’t know anything.

109
00:05:45,034 --> 00:05:46,767
I’m awfully sorry for you.

110
00:05:47,067 --> 00:05:51,867
if I go to the Emerald City with you, that Oz would give me some brains?

111
00:05:52,167 --> 00:05:54,700
but you may come with me, if you like.

112
00:05:55,000 --> 00:06:00,133
If Oz will not give you any brains you will be no worse off than you are now.

113
00:06:00,433 --> 00:06:05,766
I don’t mind my legs and arms and body being stuffed, because I cannot get hurt.

114
00:06:06,066 --> 00:06:10,799
If anyone treads on my toes or sticks a pin into me, it doesn’t matter,

115
00:06:11,099 --> 00:06:12,432
for I can’t feel it.

116
00:06:12,732 --> 00:06:15,598
But I do not want people to call me a fool,

117
00:06:15,898 --> 00:06:20,964
and if my head stays stuffed with straw instead of with brains, as yours is,

118
00:06:21,264 --> 00:06:23,330
how am I ever to know anything?

119
00:06:23,630 --> 00:06:27,763
If you will come with me I’ll ask Oz to do all he can for you.

120
00:06:28,063 --> 00:06:29,263
He never bites.

121
00:06:29,563 --> 00:06:31,163
He can’t hurt the straw.

122
00:06:31,463 --> 00:06:33,863
Do let me carry that basket for you.

123
00:06:34,163 --> 00:06:37,029
I shall not mind it, for I can’t get tired.

124
00:06:37,329 --> 00:06:40,795
There is only one thing in the world I am afraid of.

125
00:06:41,095 --> 00:06:42,295
What is that?

126
00:06:42,595 --> 00:06:44,795
the Munchkin farmer who made you?

127
00:06:45,095 --> 00:06:46,495
it’s a lighted match.

128
00:06:46,795 --> 00:06:50,995
and it is a lucky thing I am not, for my mouth is only painted,

129
00:06:51,295 --> 00:06:54,495
and if I should cut a hole in it so I could eat,

130
00:06:54,795 --> 00:06:57,661
the straw I am stuffed with would come out,

131
00:06:57,961 --> 00:07:00,761
and that would spoil the shape of my head.

132
00:07:01,061 --> 00:07:07,394
I cannot understand why you should wish to leave this beautiful country and go back to the dry,

133
00:07:07,694 --> 00:07:09,494
gray place you call Kansas.

134
00:07:09,794 --> 00:07:12,727
No matter how dreary and gray our homes are,

135
00:07:13,027 --> 00:07:18,293
we people of flesh and blood would rather live there than in any other country,

136
00:07:18,593 --> 00:07:20,193
be it ever so beautiful.

137
00:07:20,493 --> 00:07:22,359
There is no place like home.

138
00:07:22,659 --> 00:07:25,925
If your heads were stuffed with straw, like mine,

139
00:07:26,225 --> 00:07:29,691
you would probably all live in the beautiful places,

140
00:07:29,991 --> 00:07:32,924
and then Kansas would have no people at all.

141
00:07:33,224 --> 00:07:36,424
It is fortunate for Kansas that you have brains.

142
00:07:36,724 --> 00:07:39,924
Won’t you tell me a story, while we are resting?

143
00:07:40,224 --> 00:07:43,224
After the crows had gone I thought this over,

144
00:07:43,524 --> 00:07:46,724
and decided I would try hard to get some brains.

145
00:07:47,024 --> 00:07:50,757
By good luck you came along and pulled me off the stake,

146
00:07:51,057 --> 00:07:57,923
and from what you say I am sure the Great Oz will give me brains as soon as we get to the Emerald City.

147
00:07:58,223 --> 00:08:00,623
since you seem anxious to have them.

148
00:08:00,923 --> 00:08:04,789
It is such an uncomfortable feeling to know one is a fool.

149
00:08:05,089 --> 00:08:06,289
let us go.

150
00:08:06,589 --> 00:08:10,322
and as the Emerald City is at the other end of the road,

151
00:08:10,622 --> 00:08:12,755
we must go wherever it leads us.

152
00:08:13,055 --> 00:08:17,588
If it required brains to figure it out, I never should have said it.

153
00:08:17,888 --> 00:08:22,354
you must tell me; for it is very uncomfortable walking in the dark.

154
00:08:22,654 --> 00:08:24,454
built of logs and branches.

155
00:08:24,754 --> 00:08:25,954
Shall we go there?

156
00:08:26,254 --> 00:08:27,520
I am all tired out.

157
00:08:27,820 --> 00:08:29,286
Why do you want water?

158
00:08:29,586 --> 00:08:33,786
To wash my face clean after the dust of the road, and to drink,

159
00:08:34,086 --> 00:08:37,086
so the dry bread will not stick in my throat.

160
00:08:37,386 --> 00:08:39,919
for you must sleep, and eat and drink.

161
00:08:40,219 --> 00:08:41,885
However, you have brains,

162
00:08:42,185 --> 00:08:46,251
and it is worth a lot of bother to be able to think properly.

163
00:08:46,551 --> 00:08:47,751
What was that?

164
00:08:48,051 --> 00:08:49,517
but we can go and see.

165
00:08:49,817 --> 00:08:51,017
Did you groan?

166
00:08:51,317 --> 00:08:53,983
I’ve been groaning for more than a year,

167
00:08:54,283 --> 00:08:57,949
and no one has ever heard me before or come to help me.

168
00:08:58,249 --> 00:08:59,715
What can I do for you?

169
00:09:00,015 --> 00:09:03,748
They are rusted so badly that I cannot move them at all;

170
00:09:04,048 --> 00:09:07,448
if I am well oiled I shall soon be all right again.

171
00:09:07,748 --> 00:09:11,081
You will find an oil-can on a shelf in my cottage.

172
00:09:11,381 --> 00:09:12,847
Where are your joints?

173
00:09:13,147 --> 00:09:17,147
I have been holding that axe in the air ever since I rusted,

174
00:09:17,447 --> 00:09:20,580
and I’m glad to be able to put it down at last.

175
00:09:20,880 --> 00:09:25,880
Now, if you will oil the joints of my legs, I shall be all right once more.

176
00:09:26,180 --> 00:09:28,580
so you have certainly saved my life.

177
00:09:28,880 --> 00:09:30,880
How did you happen to be here?

178
00:09:31,180 --> 00:09:34,446
and we stopped at your cottage to pass the night.

179
00:09:34,746 --> 00:09:36,479
Why do you wish to see Oz?

180
00:09:36,779 --> 00:09:39,445
Do you suppose Oz could give me a heart?

181
00:09:39,745 --> 00:09:43,211
It would be as easy as to give the Scarecrow brains.

182
00:09:43,511 --> 00:09:46,444
So, if you will allow me to join your party,

183
00:09:46,744 --> 00:09:50,544
I will also go to the Emerald City and ask Oz to help me.

184
00:09:50,844 --> 00:09:56,377
if I should get caught in the rain, and rust again, I would need the oil-can badly.

185
00:09:56,677 --> 00:09:59,077
Why didn’t you walk around the hole?

186
00:09:59,377 --> 00:10:02,043
My head is stuffed with straw, you know,

187
00:10:02,343 --> 00:10:06,343
and that is why I am going to Oz to ask him for some brains.

188
00:10:06,643 --> 00:10:10,643
But, after all, brains are not the best things in the world.

189
00:10:10,943 --> 00:10:12,143
Have you any?

190
00:10:12,443 --> 00:10:16,976
But once I had brains, and a heart also; so, having tried them both,

191
00:10:17,276 --> 00:10:19,542
I should much rather have a heart.

192
00:10:19,842 --> 00:10:21,042
And why is that?

193
00:10:21,342 --> 00:10:24,608
I will tell you my story, and then you will know.

194
00:10:24,908 --> 00:10:27,708
I shall ask for brains instead of a heart;

195
00:10:28,008 --> 00:10:32,274
for a fool would not know what to do with a heart if he had one.

196
00:10:32,574 --> 00:10:37,840
for brains do not make one happy, and happiness is the best thing in the world.

197
00:10:38,140 --> 00:10:40,273
before we are out of the forest?

198
00:10:40,573 --> 00:10:42,039
What will protect him?

199
00:10:42,339 --> 00:10:44,205
Don’t you dare to bite Toto!

200
00:10:44,505 --> 00:10:48,371
You ought to be ashamed of yourself, a big beast like you,

201
00:10:48,671 --> 00:10:50,404
to bite a poor little dog!

202
00:10:50,704 --> 00:10:52,904
You are nothing but a big coward.

203
00:10:53,204 --> 00:10:54,604
I’ve always known it.

204
00:10:54,904 --> 00:10:56,370
But how can I help it?

205
00:10:56,670 --> 00:10:58,203
I don’t know, I’m sure.

206
00:10:58,503 --> 00:11:02,836
To think of your striking a stuffed man, like the poor Scarecrow!

207
00:11:03,136 --> 00:11:04,336
Is he stuffed?

208
00:11:04,636 --> 00:11:07,569
It astonished me to see him whirl around so.

209
00:11:07,869 --> 00:11:09,869
Is the other one stuffed also?

210
00:11:10,169 --> 00:11:11,369
he’s made of tin.

211
00:11:11,669 --> 00:11:16,669
When they scratched against the tin it made a cold shiver run down my back.

212
00:11:16,969 --> 00:11:20,169
What is that little animal you are so tender of?

213
00:11:20,469 --> 00:11:22,469
Is he made of tin, or stuffed?

214
00:11:22,769 --> 00:11:27,635
He’s a curious animal and seems remarkably small, now that I look at him.

215
00:11:27,935 --> 00:11:29,535
What makes you a coward?

216
00:11:29,835 --> 00:11:31,235
But that isn’t right.

217
00:11:31,535 --> 00:11:35,135
It is my great sorrow, and makes my life very unhappy.

218
00:11:35,435 --> 00:11:39,368
But whenever there is danger, my heart begins to beat fast.

219
00:11:39,668 --> 00:11:43,201
you ought to be glad, for it proves you have a heart.

220
00:11:43,501 --> 00:11:47,567
For my part, I have no heart; so I cannot have heart disease.

221
00:11:47,867 --> 00:11:50,733
if I had no heart I should not be a coward.

222
00:11:51,033 --> 00:11:52,233
Have you brains?

223
00:11:52,533 --> 00:11:53,733
I suppose so.

224
00:11:54,033 --> 00:11:56,299
for my head is stuffed with straw.

225
00:11:56,599 --> 00:11:59,132
Do you think Oz could give me courage?

226
00:11:59,432 --> 00:12:03,298
for my life is simply unbearable without a bit of courage.

227
00:12:03,598 --> 00:12:07,131
for you will help to keep away the other wild beasts.

228
00:12:07,431 --> 00:12:13,897
It seems to me they must be more cowardly than you are if they allow you to scare them so easily.

229
00:12:14,197 --> 00:12:16,597
but that doesn’t make me any braver,

230
00:12:16,897 --> 00:12:21,097
and as long as I know myself to be a coward I shall be unhappy.

231
00:12:21,397 --> 00:12:22,797
to look where I step.

232
00:12:23,097 --> 00:12:27,697
For if I should kill another bug or beetle I should surely cry again,

233
00:12:27,997 --> 00:12:31,197
and crying rusts my jaws so that I cannot speak.

234
00:12:31,497 --> 00:12:36,430
have something to guide you, and need never do wrong; but I have no heart,

235
00:12:36,730 --> 00:12:38,730
and so I must be very careful.

236
00:12:39,030 --> 00:12:42,896
When Oz gives me a heart of course I needn’t mind so much.

237
00:12:43,196 --> 00:12:46,529
I will go into the forest and kill a deer for you.

238
00:12:46,829 --> 00:12:48,762
You can roast it by the fire,

239
00:12:49,062 --> 00:12:53,195
since your tastes are so peculiar that you prefer cooked food,

240
00:12:53,495 --> 00:12:56,495
and then you will have a very good breakfast.

241
00:12:56,795 --> 00:13:00,128
I should certainly weep if you killed a poor deer,

242
00:13:00,428 --> 00:13:02,694
and then my jaws would rust again.

243
00:13:02,994 --> 00:13:04,194
What shall we do?

244
00:13:04,494 --> 00:13:06,560
We cannot fly, that is certain.

245
00:13:06,860 --> 00:13:10,060
Neither can we climb down into this great ditch.

246
00:13:10,360 --> 00:13:14,626
Therefore, if we cannot jump over it, we must stop where we are.

247
00:13:14,926 --> 00:13:18,792
for you can carry us all over on your back, one at a time.

248
00:13:19,092 --> 00:13:20,292
Who will go first?

249
00:13:20,592 --> 00:13:25,992
for, if you found that you could not jump over the gulf, Dorothy would be killed,

250
00:13:26,292 --> 00:13:29,692
or the Tin Woodman badly dented on the rocks below.

251
00:13:29,992 --> 00:13:33,458
But if I am on your back it will not matter so much,

252
00:13:33,758 --> 00:13:36,291
for the fall would not hurt me at all.

253
00:13:36,591 --> 00:13:39,791
but I suppose there is nothing to do but try it.

254
00:13:40,091 --> 00:13:43,224
So get on my back and we will make the attempt.

255
00:13:43,524 --> 00:13:45,324
Why don’t you run and jump?

256
00:13:45,624 --> 00:13:47,090
What are the Kalidahs?

257
00:13:47,390 --> 00:13:53,723
and with claws so long and sharp that they could tear me in two as easily as I could kill Toto.

258
00:13:54,023 --> 00:13:56,423
I’m terribly afraid of the Kalidahs.

259
00:13:56,723 --> 00:13:58,656
They must be dreadful beasts.

260
00:13:58,956 --> 00:14:02,289
Here is a great tree, standing close to the ditch.

261
00:14:02,589 --> 00:14:07,655
If the Tin Woodman can chop it down, so that it will fall to the other side,

262
00:14:07,955 --> 00:14:09,888
we can walk across it easily.

263
00:14:10,188 --> 00:14:14,921
One would almost suspect you had brains in your head, instead of straw.

264
00:14:15,221 --> 00:14:16,687
They are the Kalidahs!

265
00:14:16,987 --> 00:14:18,187
Let us cross over.

266
00:14:18,487 --> 00:14:23,487
We are lost, for they will surely tear us to pieces with their sharp claws.

267
00:14:23,787 --> 00:14:28,520
But stand close behind me, and I will fight them as long as I am alive.

268
00:14:28,820 --> 00:14:30,020
Wait a minute!

269
00:14:30,320 --> 00:14:34,986
I see we are going to live a little while longer, and I am glad of it,

270
00:14:35,286 --> 00:14:39,152
for it must be a very uncomfortable thing not to be alive.

271
00:14:39,452 --> 00:14:43,985
Those creatures frightened me so badly that my heart is beating yet.

272
00:14:44,285 --> 00:14:46,218
I wish I had a heart to beat.

273
00:14:46,518 --> 00:14:48,451
How shall we cross the river?

274
00:14:48,751 --> 00:14:53,551
The Tin Woodman must build us a raft, so we can float to the other side.

275
00:14:53,851 --> 00:15:00,651
for if we cannot get to the land we shall be carried into the country of the Wicked Witch of the West,

276
00:15:00,951 --> 00:15:04,084
and she will enchant us and make us her slaves.

277
00:15:04,384 --> 00:15:07,250
Then, I was stuck on a pole in a cornfield,

278
00:15:07,550 --> 00:15:11,283
where I could make-believe scare the crows, at any rate.

279
00:15:11,583 --> 00:15:17,183
But surely there is no use for a Scarecrow stuck on a pole in the middle of a river.

280
00:15:17,483 --> 00:15:21,016
I am afraid I shall never have any brains, after all!

281
00:15:21,316 --> 00:15:23,582
Something must be done to save us.

282
00:15:23,882 --> 00:15:27,815
I think I can swim to the shore and pull the raft after me,

283
00:15:28,115 --> 00:15:31,381
if you will only hold fast to the tip of my tail.

284
00:15:31,681 --> 00:15:33,081
What shall we do now?

285
00:15:33,381 --> 00:15:35,181
What can we do to save him?

286
00:15:35,481 --> 00:15:37,881
Who are you and where are you going?

287
00:15:38,181 --> 00:15:42,447
and these are my friends, the Tin Woodman and the Cowardly Lion;

288
00:15:42,747 --> 00:15:45,213
and we are going to the Emerald City.

289
00:15:45,513 --> 00:15:50,646
but we have lost the Scarecrow, and are wondering how we shall get him again.

290
00:15:50,946 --> 00:15:52,146
Where is he?

291
00:15:52,446 --> 00:15:56,912
for he is stuffed with straw; and if you will bring him back to us,

292
00:15:57,212 --> 00:15:59,945
we shall thank you ever and ever so much.

293
00:16:00,245 --> 00:16:05,778
but if I find he is too heavy to carry I shall have to drop him in the river again.

294
00:16:06,078 --> 00:16:07,278
Tol-de-ri-de-oh!

295
00:16:07,578 --> 00:16:09,444
but the kind Stork saved me,

296
00:16:09,744 --> 00:16:15,944
and if I ever get any brains I shall find the Stork again and do her some kindness in return.

297
00:16:16,244 --> 00:16:18,910
I always like to help anyone in trouble.

298
00:16:19,210 --> 00:16:23,476
But I must go now, for my babies are waiting in the nest for me.

299
00:16:23,776 --> 00:16:28,042
I hope you will find the Emerald City and that Oz will help you.

300
00:16:28,342 --> 00:16:29,808
Aren’t they beautiful?

301
00:16:30,108 --> 00:16:33,708
When I have brains, I shall probably like them better.

302
00:16:34,008 --> 00:16:36,141
They seem so helpless and frail.

303
00:16:36,441 --> 00:16:39,907
But there are none in the forest so bright as these.

304
00:16:40,207 --> 00:16:41,407
What shall we do?

305
00:16:41,707 --> 00:16:44,573
The smell of the flowers is killing us all.

306
00:16:44,873 --> 00:16:49,606
I myself can scarcely keep my eyes open, and the dog is asleep already.

307
00:16:49,906 --> 00:16:53,706
and get out of this deadly flower bed as soon as you can.

308
00:16:54,006 --> 00:16:56,539
We will bring the little girl with us,

309
00:16:56,839 --> 00:17:00,839
but if you should fall asleep you are too big to be carried.

310
00:17:01,139 --> 00:17:03,339
for he is much too heavy to lift.

311
00:17:03,639 --> 00:17:06,505
We must leave him here to sleep on forever,

312
00:17:06,805 --> 00:17:10,805
and perhaps he will dream that he has found courage at last.

313
00:17:11,105 --> 00:17:14,638
The Lion was a very good comrade for one so cowardly.

314
00:17:14,938 --> 00:17:16,138
But let us go on.

315
00:17:16,438 --> 00:17:20,438
for we have come nearly as far as the river carried us away.

316
00:17:20,738 --> 00:17:21,938
Oh, thank you!

317
00:17:22,238 --> 00:17:25,038
Thank you ever so much for saving my life.

318
00:17:25,338 --> 00:17:30,871
I have no heart, you know, so I am careful to help all those who may need a friend,

319
00:17:31,171 --> 00:17:33,704
even if it happens to be only a mouse.

320
00:17:34,004 --> 00:17:35,204
Only a mouse!

321
00:17:35,504 --> 00:17:38,837
Why, I am a Queen—the Queen of all the Field Mice!

322
00:17:39,137 --> 00:17:42,403
Oh, your Majesty, we thought you would be killed!

323
00:17:42,703 --> 00:17:45,836
How did you manage to escape the great Wildcat?

324
00:17:46,136 --> 00:17:48,602
killed the Wildcat and saved my life.

325
00:17:48,902 --> 00:17:53,235
So hereafter you must all serve him, and obey his slightest wish.

326
00:17:53,535 --> 00:17:54,735
Come back!

327
00:17:55,035 --> 00:17:56,235
Come back!

328
00:17:56,535 --> 00:17:58,135
Toto shall not hurt you.

329
00:17:58,435 --> 00:18:00,635
Are you sure he will not bite us?

330
00:18:00,935 --> 00:18:02,268
so do not be afraid.

331
00:18:02,568 --> 00:18:05,634
to repay you for saving the life of our Queen?

332
00:18:05,934 --> 00:18:11,534
Oh, yes; you can save our friend, the Cowardly Lion, who is asleep in the poppy bed.

333
00:18:11,834 --> 00:18:13,700
Why, he would eat us all up.

334
00:18:14,000 --> 00:18:15,466
this Lion is a coward.

335
00:18:15,766 --> 00:18:19,032
and he would never hurt anyone who is our friend.

336
00:18:19,332 --> 00:18:24,932
If you will help us to save him I promise that he shall treat you all with kindness.

337
00:18:25,232 --> 00:18:26,432
we trust you.

338
00:18:26,732 --> 00:18:28,132
But what shall we do?

339
00:18:28,432 --> 00:18:33,632
Are there many of these mice which call you Queen and are willing to obey you?

340
00:18:33,932 --> 00:18:37,665
Then send for them all to come here as soon as possible,

341
00:18:37,965 --> 00:18:41,031
and let each one bring a long piece of string.

342
00:18:41,331 --> 00:18:47,064
you must go to those trees by the riverside and make a truck that will carry the Lion.

343
00:18:47,364 --> 00:18:50,897
Permit me to introduce to you her Majesty, the Queen.

344
00:18:51,197 --> 00:18:56,797
come out into the field and call, and we shall hear you and come to your assistance.

345
00:18:57,097 --> 00:18:59,697
but the flowers were too strong for me.

346
00:18:59,997 --> 00:19:01,530
How did you get me out?

347
00:19:01,830 --> 00:19:05,230
I have always thought myself very big and terrible;

348
00:19:05,530 --> 00:19:09,396
yet such little things as flowers came near to killing me,

349
00:19:09,696 --> 00:19:13,029
and such small animals as mice have saved my life.

350
00:19:13,329 --> 00:19:14,795
How strange it all is!

351
00:19:15,095 --> 00:19:17,495
But, comrades, what shall we do now?

352
00:19:17,795 --> 00:19:20,728
and then we can keep on to the Emerald City.

353
00:19:21,028 --> 00:19:24,228
and we are surely getting near the Emerald City.

354
00:19:24,528 --> 00:19:26,194
Everything is green here,

355
00:19:26,494 --> 00:19:30,894
while in the country of the Munchkins blue was the favorite color.

356
00:19:31,194 --> 00:19:35,327
But the people do not seem to be as friendly as the Munchkins,

357
00:19:35,627 --> 00:19:40,160
and I’m afraid we shall be unable to find a place to pass the night.

358
00:19:40,460 --> 00:19:42,860
and I’m sure Toto is nearly starved.

359
00:19:43,160 --> 00:19:46,693
Let us stop at the next house and talk to the people.

360
00:19:46,993 --> 00:19:51,059
What do you want, child, and why is that great Lion with you?

361
00:19:51,359 --> 00:19:56,425
and the Lion is my friend and comrade, and would not hurt you for the world.

362
00:19:56,725 --> 00:19:57,925
Is he tame?

363
00:19:58,225 --> 00:20:00,225
and he is a great coward, too.

364
00:20:00,525 --> 00:20:03,858
He will be more afraid of you than you are of him.

365
00:20:04,158 --> 00:20:06,558
if that is the case you may come in,

366
00:20:06,858 --> 00:20:10,391
and I will give you some supper and a place to sleep.

367
00:20:10,691 --> 00:20:12,291
Where are you all going?

368
00:20:12,591 --> 00:20:13,924
to see the Great Oz.

369
00:20:14,224 --> 00:20:15,424
Oh, indeed!

370
00:20:15,724 --> 00:20:17,990
Are you sure that Oz will see you?

371
00:20:18,290 --> 00:20:22,623
Why, it is said that he never lets anyone come into his presence.

372
00:20:22,923 --> 00:20:25,789
I have been to the Emerald City many times,

373
00:20:26,089 --> 00:20:28,889
and it is a beautiful and wonderful place;

374
00:20:29,189 --> 00:20:32,655
but I have never been permitted to see the Great Oz,

375
00:20:32,955 --> 00:20:36,421
nor do I know of any living person who has seen him.

376
00:20:36,721 --> 00:20:38,121
Does he never go out?

377
00:20:38,421 --> 00:20:42,487
He sits day after day in the great Throne Room of his Palace,

378
00:20:42,787 --> 00:20:46,853
and even those who wait upon him do not see him face to face.

379
00:20:47,153 --> 00:20:48,353
What is he like?

380
00:20:48,653 --> 00:20:53,053
You see, Oz is a Great Wizard, and can take on any form he wishes.

381
00:20:53,353 --> 00:20:58,553
So that some say he looks like a bird; and some say he looks like an elephant;

382
00:20:58,853 --> 00:21:01,053
and some say he looks like a cat.

383
00:21:01,353 --> 00:21:05,086
To others he appears as a beautiful fairy, or a brownie,

384
00:21:05,386 --> 00:21:07,919
or in any other form that pleases him.

385
00:21:08,219 --> 00:21:13,419
But who the real Oz is, when he is in his own form, no living person can tell.

386
00:21:13,719 --> 00:21:16,452
but we must try, in some way, to see him,

387
00:21:16,752 --> 00:21:19,818
or we shall have made our journey for nothing.

388
00:21:20,118 --> 00:21:22,718
Why do you wish to see the terrible Oz?

389
00:21:23,018 --> 00:21:25,218
He has more brains than he needs.

390
00:21:25,518 --> 00:21:29,851
for Oz has a large collection of hearts, of all sizes and shapes.

391
00:21:30,151 --> 00:21:34,884
which he has covered with a golden plate, to keep it from running over.

392
00:21:35,184 --> 00:21:37,384
He will be glad to give you some.

393
00:21:37,684 --> 00:21:38,884
Where is Kansas?

394
00:21:39,184 --> 00:21:42,317
but it is my home, and I’m sure it’s somewhere.

395
00:21:42,617 --> 00:21:43,817
Very likely.

396
00:21:44,117 --> 00:21:48,583
Well, Oz can do anything; so I suppose he will find Kansas for you.

397
00:21:48,883 --> 00:21:53,149
But first you must get to see him, and that will be a hard task;

398
00:21:53,449 --> 00:21:58,849
for the Great Wizard does not like to see anyone, and he usually has his own way.

399
00:21:59,149 --> 00:22:00,549
But what do YOU want?

400
00:22:00,849 --> 00:22:03,315
What do you wish in the Emerald City?

401
00:22:03,615 --> 00:22:05,481
He is powerful and terrible,

402
00:22:05,781 --> 00:22:12,181
and if you come on an idle or foolish errand to bother the wise reflections of the Great Wizard,

403
00:22:12,481 --> 00:22:15,947
he might be angry and destroy you all in an instant.

404
00:22:16,247 --> 00:22:17,447
it is important.

405
00:22:17,747 --> 00:22:20,880
And we have been told that Oz is a good Wizard.

406
00:22:21,180 --> 00:22:24,246
and he rules the Emerald City wisely and well.

407
00:22:24,546 --> 00:22:29,079
But to those who are not honest, or who approach him from curiosity,

408
00:22:29,379 --> 00:22:33,712
he is most terrible, and few have ever dared ask to see his face.

409
00:22:34,012 --> 00:22:36,078
I am the Guardian of the Gates,

410
00:22:36,378 --> 00:22:41,111
and since you demand to see the Great Oz I must take you to his Palace.

411
00:22:41,411 --> 00:22:44,144
But first you must put on the spectacles.

412
00:22:44,444 --> 00:22:51,110
Because if you did not wear spectacles the brightness and glory of the Emerald City would blind you.

413
00:22:51,410 --> 00:22:55,876
Even those who live in the City must wear spectacles night and day.

414
00:22:56,176 --> 00:23:01,176
They are all locked on, for Oz so ordered it when the City was first built,

415
00:23:01,476 --> 00:23:04,542
and I have the only key that will unlock them.

416
00:23:04,842 --> 00:23:07,242
and they demand to see the Great Oz.

417
00:23:07,542 --> 00:23:10,008
and I will carry your message to him.

418
00:23:10,308 --> 00:23:17,108
Please make yourselves comfortable while I go to the door of the Throne Room and tell Oz you are here.

419
00:23:17,408 --> 00:23:18,608
Have you seen Oz?

420
00:23:18,908 --> 00:23:20,374
I have never seen him.

421
00:23:20,674 --> 00:23:25,540
But I spoke to him as he sat behind his screen and gave him your message.

422
00:23:25,840 --> 00:23:29,573
He said he will grant you an audience, if you so desire;

423
00:23:29,873 --> 00:23:33,206
but each one of you must enter his presence alone,

424
00:23:33,506 --> 00:23:35,839
and he will admit but one each day.

425
00:23:36,139 --> 00:23:40,205
Therefore, as you must remain in the Palace for several days,

426
00:23:40,505 --> 00:23:45,838
I will have you shown to rooms where you may rest in comfort after your journey.

427
00:23:46,138 --> 00:23:47,738
that is very kind of Oz.

428
00:23:48,038 --> 00:23:50,704
Follow me and I will show you your room.

429
00:23:51,004 --> 00:23:53,870
and if you wish for anything ring the bell.

430
00:23:54,170 --> 00:23:56,703
Oz will send for you tomorrow morning.

431
00:23:57,003 --> 00:24:01,136
Are you really going to look upon the face of Oz the Terrible?

432
00:24:01,436 --> 00:24:02,636
if he will see me.

433
00:24:02,936 --> 00:24:06,669
although he does not like to have people ask to see him.

434
00:24:06,969 --> 00:24:12,435
Indeed, at first he was angry and said I should send you back where you came from.

435
00:24:12,735 --> 00:24:15,268
Then he asked me what you looked like,

436
00:24:15,568 --> 00:24:20,034
and when I mentioned your silver shoes he was very much interested.

437
00:24:20,334 --> 00:24:23,867
At last I told him about the mark upon your forehead,

438
00:24:24,167 --> 00:24:27,500
and he decided he would admit you to his presence.

439
00:24:27,800 --> 00:24:29,066
That is the signal.

440
00:24:29,366 --> 00:24:31,966
You must go into the Throne Room alone.

441
00:24:32,266 --> 00:24:34,399
I am Oz, the Great and Terrible.

442
00:24:34,699 --> 00:24:37,099
Who are you, and why do you seek me?

443
00:24:37,399 --> 00:24:39,599
I am Dorothy, the Small and Meek.

444
00:24:39,899 --> 00:24:41,765
I have come to you for help.

445
00:24:42,065 --> 00:24:44,398
Where did you get the silver shoes?

446
00:24:44,698 --> 00:24:47,764
Where did you get the mark upon your forehead?

447
00:24:48,064 --> 00:24:49,797
What do you wish me to do?

448
00:24:50,097 --> 00:24:53,763
I don’t like your country, although it is so beautiful.

449
00:24:54,063 --> 00:24:59,129
And I am sure Aunt Em will be dreadfully worried over my being away so long.

450
00:24:59,429 --> 00:25:01,362
Why should I do this for you?

451
00:25:01,662 --> 00:25:04,128
Because you are strong and I am weak;

452
00:25:04,428 --> 00:25:08,361
because you are a Great Wizard and I am only a little girl.

453
00:25:08,661 --> 00:25:09,994
I could not help it.

454
00:25:10,294 --> 00:25:12,027
I will give you my answer.

455
00:25:12,327 --> 00:25:18,927
You have no right to expect me to send you back to Kansas unless you do something for me in return.

456
00:25:19,227 --> 00:25:23,027
In this country everyone must pay for everything he gets.

457
00:25:23,327 --> 00:25:29,660
If you wish me to use my magic power to send you home again you must do something for me first.

458
00:25:29,960 --> 00:25:31,826
Help me and I will help you.

459
00:25:32,126 --> 00:25:33,326
What must I do?

460
00:25:33,626 --> 00:25:34,826
But I cannot!

461
00:25:35,126 --> 00:25:39,326
You killed the Witch of the East and you wear the silver shoes,

462
00:25:39,626 --> 00:25:41,492
which bear a powerful charm.

463
00:25:41,792 --> 00:25:45,525
There is now but one Wicked Witch left in all this land,

464
00:25:45,825 --> 00:25:51,358
and when you can tell me she is dead I will send you back to Kansas—but not before.

465
00:25:51,658 --> 00:25:55,324
Even if I wanted to, how could I kill the Wicked Witch?

466
00:25:55,624 --> 00:25:59,690
If you, who are Great and Terrible, cannot kill her yourself,

467
00:25:59,990 --> 00:26:01,990
how do you expect me to do it?

468
00:26:02,290 --> 00:26:03,756
but that is my answer,

469
00:26:04,056 --> 00:26:09,056
and until the Wicked Witch dies you will not see your uncle and aunt again.

470
00:26:09,356 --> 00:26:14,489
Remember that the Witch is Wicked—tremendously Wicked—and ought to be killed.

471
00:26:14,789 --> 00:26:19,389
Now go, and do not ask to see me again until you have done your task.

472
00:26:19,689 --> 00:26:24,889
for Oz will not send me home until I have killed the Wicked Witch of the West;

473
00:26:25,189 --> 00:26:26,789
and that I can never do.

474
00:26:27,089 --> 00:26:29,622
Come with me, for Oz has sent for you.

475
00:26:29,922 --> 00:26:32,055
I am Oz, the Great and Terrible.

476
00:26:32,355 --> 00:26:34,755
Who are you, and why do you seek me?

477
00:26:35,055 --> 00:26:37,855
I am only a Scarecrow, stuffed with straw.

478
00:26:38,155 --> 00:26:39,955
Therefore I have no brains,

479
00:26:40,255 --> 00:26:45,521
and I come to you praying that you will put brains in my head instead of straw,

480
00:26:45,821 --> 00:26:50,221
so that I may become as much a man as any other in your dominions.

481
00:26:50,521 --> 00:26:52,454
Why should I do this for you?

482
00:26:52,754 --> 00:26:54,687
but this much I will promise.

483
00:26:54,987 --> 00:26:58,520
If you will kill for me the Wicked Witch of the West,

484
00:26:58,820 --> 00:27:01,686
I will bestow upon you a great many brains,

485
00:27:01,986 --> 00:27:06,986
and such good brains that you will be the wisest man in all the Land of Oz.

486
00:27:07,286 --> 00:27:09,086
I don’t care who kills her.

487
00:27:09,386 --> 00:27:12,652
But until she is dead I will not grant your wish.

488
00:27:12,952 --> 00:27:14,152
Now go,

489
00:27:14,452 --> 00:27:19,785
and do not seek me again until you have earned the brains you so greatly desire.

490
00:27:20,085 --> 00:27:23,085
she needs a heart as much as the Tin Woodman.

491
00:27:23,385 --> 00:27:24,718
Oz has sent for you.

492
00:27:25,018 --> 00:27:26,218
Follow me.

493
00:27:26,518 --> 00:27:30,384
if it is the head, I am sure I shall not be given a heart,

494
00:27:30,684 --> 00:27:35,350
since a head has no heart of its own and therefore cannot feel for me.

495
00:27:35,650 --> 00:27:39,516
But if it is the lovely Lady I shall beg hard for a heart,

496
00:27:39,816 --> 00:27:43,549
for all ladies are themselves said to be kindly hearted.

497
00:27:43,849 --> 00:27:46,249
Who are you, and why do you seek me?

498
00:27:46,549 --> 00:27:48,682
I am a Woodman, and made of tin.

499
00:27:48,982 --> 00:27:51,848
Therefore I have no heart, and cannot love.

500
00:27:52,148 --> 00:27:56,214
I pray you to give me a heart that I may be as other men are.

501
00:27:56,514 --> 00:27:57,914
Why should I do this?

502
00:27:58,214 --> 00:28:01,347
If you indeed desire a heart, you must earn it.

503
00:28:01,647 --> 00:28:03,980
When the Witch is dead, come to me,

504
00:28:04,280 --> 00:28:10,480
and I will then give you the biggest and kindest and most loving heart in all the Land of Oz.

505
00:28:10,780 --> 00:28:12,913
I am Oz, the Great and Terrible.

506
00:28:13,213 --> 00:28:15,613
Who are you, and why do you seek me?

507
00:28:15,913 --> 00:28:18,779
I am a Cowardly Lion, afraid of everything.

508
00:28:19,079 --> 00:28:22,145
I came to you to beg that you give me courage,

509
00:28:22,445 --> 00:28:26,911
so that in reality I may become the King of Beasts, as men call me.

510
00:28:27,211 --> 00:28:29,211
Why should I give you courage?

511
00:28:29,511 --> 00:28:32,511
Bring me proof that the Wicked Witch is dead,

512
00:28:32,811 --> 00:28:35,477
and that moment I will give you courage.

513
00:28:35,777 --> 00:28:39,577
But as long as the Witch lives, you must remain a coward.

514
00:28:39,877 --> 00:28:41,277
What shall we do now?

515
00:28:41,577 --> 00:28:46,377
and that is to go to the land of the Winkies, seek out the Wicked Witch,

516
00:28:46,677 --> 00:28:47,877
and destroy her.

517
00:28:48,177 --> 00:28:49,643
But suppose we cannot?

518
00:28:49,943 --> 00:28:51,143
Be careful!

519
00:28:51,443 --> 00:28:55,176
The tears will fall on your green silk gown and spot it.

520
00:28:55,476 --> 00:29:00,142
I suppose we must try it; but I am sure I do not want to kill anybody,

521
00:29:00,442 --> 00:29:02,175
even to see Aunt Em again.

522
00:29:02,475 --> 00:29:06,275
but I shall not be of much help to you, I am such a fool.

523
00:29:06,575 --> 00:29:09,508
but if you go I certainly shall go with you.

524
00:29:09,808 --> 00:29:13,074
Which road leads to the Wicked Witch of the West?

525
00:29:13,374 --> 00:29:15,640
No one ever wishes to go that way.

526
00:29:15,940 --> 00:29:17,940
How, then, are we to find her?

527
00:29:18,240 --> 00:29:23,240
for when she knows you are in the country of the Winkies she will find you,

528
00:29:23,540 --> 00:29:25,406
and make you all her slaves.

529
00:29:25,706 --> 00:29:27,506
for we mean to destroy her.

530
00:29:27,806 --> 00:29:30,272
No one has ever destroyed her before,

531
00:29:30,572 --> 00:29:35,638
so I naturally thought she would make slaves of you, as she has of the rest.

532
00:29:35,938 --> 00:29:41,404
But take care; for she is wicked and fierce, and may not allow you to destroy her.

533
00:29:41,704 --> 00:29:46,370
Keep to the West, where the sun sets, and you cannot fail to find her.

534
00:29:46,670 --> 00:29:48,270
and tear them to pieces.

535
00:29:48,570 --> 00:29:51,436
Are you not going to make them your slaves?

536
00:29:51,736 --> 00:29:56,136
one is of tin, and one of straw; one is a girl and another a Lion.

537
00:29:56,436 --> 00:30:00,969
None of them is fit to work, so you may tear them into small pieces.

538
00:30:01,269 --> 00:30:04,669
so get behind me and I will meet them as they come.

539
00:30:04,969 --> 00:30:06,835
It was a good fight, friend.

540
00:30:07,135 --> 00:30:12,068
Fly at once to the strangers; peck out their eyes and tear them to pieces.

541
00:30:12,368 --> 00:30:16,901
This is my battle, so lie down beside me and you will not be harmed.

542
00:30:17,201 --> 00:30:18,867
It is only a stuffed man.

543
00:30:19,167 --> 00:30:20,833
I will peck his eyes out.

544
00:30:21,133 --> 00:30:24,066
Go to the strangers and sting them to death!

545
00:30:24,366 --> 00:30:26,432
and the bees cannot sting them.

546
00:30:26,732 --> 00:30:28,198
Ep-pe, pep-pe, kak-ke!

547
00:30:28,498 --> 00:30:30,031
Hil-lo, hol-lo, hel-lo!

548
00:30:30,331 --> 00:30:31,664
Ziz-zy, zuz-zy, zik!

549
00:30:31,964 --> 00:30:35,097
You have called us for the third and last time.

550
00:30:35,397 --> 00:30:36,730
What do you command?

551
00:30:37,030 --> 00:30:41,696
Bring that beast to me, for I have a mind to harness him like a horse,

552
00:30:41,996 --> 00:30:43,196
and make him work.

553
00:30:43,496 --> 00:30:46,296
for she is protected by the Power of Good,

554
00:30:46,596 --> 00:30:49,462
and that is greater than the Power of Evil.

555
00:30:49,762 --> 00:30:55,362
All we can do is to carry her to the castle of the Wicked Witch and leave her there.

556
00:30:55,662 --> 00:30:58,462
We have obeyed you as far as we were able.

557
00:30:58,762 --> 00:31:01,962
The Tin Woodman and the Scarecrow are destroyed,

558
00:31:02,262 --> 00:31:04,728
and the Lion is tied up in your yard.

559
00:31:05,028 --> 00:31:09,694
The little girl we dare not harm, nor the dog she carries in her arms.

560
00:31:09,994 --> 00:31:14,727
Your power over our band is now ended, and you will never see us again.

561
00:31:15,027 --> 00:31:19,960
I can still make her my slave, for she does not know how to use her power.

562
00:31:20,260 --> 00:31:24,126
Come with me; and see that you mind everything I tell you,

563
00:31:24,426 --> 00:31:27,359
for if you do not I will make an end of you,

564
00:31:27,659 --> 00:31:30,725
as I did of the Tin Woodman and the Scarecrow.

565
00:31:31,025 --> 00:31:32,225
I can starve you.

566
00:31:32,525 --> 00:31:36,058
You shall have nothing to eat until you do as I wish.

567
00:31:36,358 --> 00:31:39,224
Are you ready to be harnessed like a horse?

568
00:31:39,524 --> 00:31:42,324
If you come in this yard, I will bite you.

569
00:31:42,624 --> 00:31:44,024
Give me back my shoe!

570
00:31:44,324 --> 00:31:46,790
for it is now my shoe, and not yours.

571
00:31:47,090 --> 00:31:48,823
You are a wicked creature!

572
00:31:49,123 --> 00:31:51,923
You have no right to take my shoe from me.

573
00:31:52,223 --> 00:31:55,689
and someday I shall get the other one from you, too.

574
00:31:55,989 --> 00:31:57,522
See what you have done!

575
00:31:57,822 --> 00:31:59,822
In a minute I shall melt away.

576
00:32:00,122 --> 00:32:03,122
Didn’t you know water would be the end of me?

577
00:32:03,422 --> 00:32:04,622
How should I?

578
00:32:04,922 --> 00:32:07,922
Well, in a few minutes I shall be all melted,

579
00:32:08,222 --> 00:32:10,955
and you will have the castle to yourself.

580
00:32:11,255 --> 00:32:13,188
I have been wicked in my day,

581
00:32:13,488 --> 00:32:19,954
but I never thought a little girl like you would ever be able to melt me and end my wicked deeds.

582
00:32:20,254 --> 00:32:21,520
Look out—here I go!

583
00:32:21,820 --> 00:32:23,420
I should be quite happy.

584
00:32:23,720 --> 00:32:26,320
Don’t you suppose we could rescue them?

585
00:32:26,620 --> 00:32:28,820
Are any of your people tinsmiths?

586
00:32:29,120 --> 00:32:32,720
Can you straighten out those dents in the Tin Woodman,

587
00:32:33,020 --> 00:32:38,286
and bend him back into shape again, and solder him together where he is broken?

588
00:32:38,586 --> 00:32:40,186
I should be quite happy.

589
00:32:40,486 --> 00:32:44,686
I’ll chop it down, and then we can get the Scarecrow’s clothes.

590
00:32:44,986 --> 00:32:47,986
We must go back to Oz, and claim his promise.

591
00:32:48,286 --> 00:32:50,219
at last I shall get my heart.

592
00:32:50,519 --> 00:32:53,652
Oh, let us start for the Emerald City tomorrow!

593
00:32:53,952 --> 00:32:57,085
I am sure we shall sometime come to some place.

594
00:32:57,385 --> 00:33:01,518
and unless we find it again in time to reach the Emerald City,

595
00:33:01,818 --> 00:33:03,684
I shall never get my brains.

596
00:33:03,984 --> 00:33:07,450
It seems to me I can scarcely wait till I get to Oz,

597
00:33:07,750 --> 00:33:10,883
and you must admit this is a very long journey.

598
00:33:11,183 --> 00:33:16,516
I haven’t the courage to keep tramping forever, without getting anywhere at all.

599
00:33:16,816 --> 00:33:20,549
They could probably tell us the way to the Emerald City.

600
00:33:20,849 --> 00:33:23,182
Why didn’t we think of that before?

601
00:33:23,482 --> 00:33:25,415
What can I do for my friends?

602
00:33:25,715 --> 00:33:28,515
Can you tell us where the Emerald City is?

603
00:33:28,815 --> 00:33:33,815
but it is a great way off, for you have had it at your backs all this time.

604
00:33:34,115 --> 00:33:39,115
Why don’t you use the charm of the Cap, and call the Winged Monkeys to you?

605
00:33:39,415 --> 00:33:43,348
They will carry you to the City of Oz in less than an hour.

606
00:33:43,648 --> 00:33:44,848
What is it?

607
00:33:45,148 --> 00:33:49,481
But if you are going to call the Winged Monkeys we must run away,

608
00:33:49,781 --> 00:33:54,181
for they are full of mischief and think it great fun to plague us.

609
00:33:54,481 --> 00:33:55,747
Won’t they hurt me?

610
00:33:56,047 --> 00:33:58,513
They must obey the wearer of the Cap.

611
00:33:58,813 --> 00:34:00,279
Ep-pe, pep-pe, kak-ke!

612
00:34:00,579 --> 00:34:01,779
What did you say?

613
00:34:02,079 --> 00:34:03,612
Hil-lo, hol-lo, hel-lo!

614
00:34:03,912 --> 00:34:05,245
Ziz-zy, zuz-zy, zik!

615
00:34:05,545 --> 00:34:06,945
What is your command?

616
00:34:07,245 --> 00:34:08,911
and we have lost our way.

617
00:34:09,211 --> 00:34:12,677
Why do you have to obey the charm of the Golden Cap?

618
00:34:12,977 --> 00:34:15,643
but as we have a long journey before us,

619
00:34:15,943 --> 00:34:19,809
I will pass the time by telling you about it, if you wish.

620
00:34:20,109 --> 00:34:21,709
And what became of them?

621
00:34:22,009 --> 00:34:28,942
This was all we ever had to do until the Golden Cap fell into the hands of the Wicked Witch of the West,

622
00:34:29,242 --> 00:34:31,375
who made us enslave the Winkies,

623
00:34:31,675 --> 00:34:35,608
and afterward drive Oz himself out of the Land of the West.

624
00:34:35,908 --> 00:34:37,774
Now the Golden Cap is yours,

625
00:34:38,074 --> 00:34:42,207
and three times you have the right to lay your wishes upon us.

626
00:34:42,507 --> 00:34:46,040
How lucky it was you brought away that wonderful Cap!

627
00:34:46,340 --> 00:34:47,606
are you back again?

628
00:34:47,906 --> 00:34:49,106
Do you not see us?

629
00:34:49,406 --> 00:34:53,739
But I thought you had gone to visit the Wicked Witch of the West.

630
00:34:54,039 --> 00:34:55,705
And she let you go again?

631
00:34:56,005 --> 00:34:57,205
Who melted her?

632
00:34:57,505 --> 00:34:58,705
Good gracious!

633
00:34:59,005 --> 00:35:01,138
I am Oz, the Great and Terrible.

634
00:35:01,438 --> 00:35:02,704
Why do you seek me?

635
00:35:03,004 --> 00:35:04,204
Where are you?

636
00:35:04,504 --> 00:35:07,770
but to the eyes of common mortals I am invisible.

637
00:35:08,070 --> 00:35:12,670
I will now seat myself upon my throne, that you may converse with me.

638
00:35:12,970 --> 00:35:15,636
We have come to claim our promise, O Oz.

639
00:35:15,936 --> 00:35:17,136
What promise?

640
00:35:17,436 --> 00:35:19,902
Is the Wicked Witch really destroyed?

641
00:35:20,202 --> 00:35:22,602
I melted her with a bucket of water.

642
00:35:22,902 --> 00:35:24,102
how sudden!

643
00:35:24,402 --> 00:35:28,735
Well, come to me tomorrow, for I must have time to think it over.

644
00:35:29,035 --> 00:35:31,301
You must keep your promises to us!

645
00:35:31,601 --> 00:35:32,801
Who are you?

646
00:35:33,101 --> 00:35:37,701
But don’t strike me—please don’t—and I’ll do anything you want me to.

647
00:35:38,001 --> 00:35:39,801
I have been making believe.

648
00:35:40,101 --> 00:35:41,301
Making believe!

649
00:35:41,601 --> 00:35:43,401
Are you not a Great Wizard?

650
00:35:43,701 --> 00:35:48,301
Don’t speak so loud, or you will be overheard—and I should be ruined.

651
00:35:48,601 --> 00:35:50,867
I’m supposed to be a Great Wizard.

652
00:35:51,167 --> 00:35:52,367
And aren’t you?

653
00:35:52,667 --> 00:35:55,867
Not a bit of it, my dear; I’m just a common man.

654
00:35:56,167 --> 00:35:57,367
you’re a humbug.

655
00:35:57,667 --> 00:35:58,867
Exactly so!

656
00:35:59,167 --> 00:36:00,367
I am a humbug.

657
00:36:00,667 --> 00:36:02,667
How shall I ever get my heart?

658
00:36:02,967 --> 00:36:04,167
Or I my courage?

659
00:36:04,467 --> 00:36:05,667
Or I my brains?

660
00:36:05,967 --> 00:36:09,100
I pray you not to speak of these little things.

661
00:36:09,400 --> 00:36:13,666
Think of me, and the terrible trouble I’m in at being found out.

662
00:36:13,966 --> 00:36:16,699
Doesn’t anyone else know you’re a humbug?

663
00:36:16,999 --> 00:36:21,932
I have fooled everyone so long that I thought I should never be found out.

664
00:36:22,232 --> 00:36:26,498
It was a great mistake my ever letting you into the Throne Room.

665
00:36:26,798 --> 00:36:29,464
Usually I will not see even my subjects,

666
00:36:29,764 --> 00:36:32,697
and so they believe I am something terrible.

667
00:36:32,997 --> 00:36:36,397
How was it that you appeared to me as a great Head?

668
00:36:36,697 --> 00:36:40,430
Step this way, please, and I will tell you all about it.

669
00:36:40,730 --> 00:36:43,796
I stood behind the screen and pulled a thread,

670
00:36:44,096 --> 00:36:46,829
to make the eyes move and the mouth open.

671
00:36:47,129 --> 00:36:48,729
But how about the voice?

672
00:36:49,029 --> 00:36:52,362
I can throw the sound of my voice wherever I wish,

673
00:36:52,662 --> 00:36:55,995
so that you thought it was coming out of the Head.

674
00:36:56,295 --> 00:36:59,495
Here are the other things I used to deceive you.

675
00:36:59,795 --> 00:37:03,795
you ought to be ashamed of yourself for being such a humbug.

676
00:37:04,095 --> 00:37:06,561
but it was the only thing I could do.

677
00:37:06,861 --> 00:37:11,861
Sit down, please, there are plenty of chairs; and I will tell you my story.

678
00:37:12,161 --> 00:37:14,627
Why, that isn’t very far from Kansas!

679
00:37:14,927 --> 00:37:17,593
When I grew up I became a ventriloquist,

680
00:37:17,893 --> 00:37:21,493
and at that I was very well trained by a great master.

681
00:37:21,793 --> 00:37:24,593
I can imitate any kind of a bird or beast.

682
00:37:24,893 --> 00:37:27,626
I tired of that, and became a balloonist.

683
00:37:27,926 --> 00:37:29,126
What is that?

684
00:37:29,426 --> 00:37:32,892
Just to amuse myself, and keep the good people busy,

685
00:37:33,192 --> 00:37:36,458
I ordered them to build this City, and my Palace;

686
00:37:36,758 --> 00:37:39,358
and they did it all willingly and well.

687
00:37:39,658 --> 00:37:41,791
But isn’t everything here green?

688
00:37:42,091 --> 00:37:46,824
Oh, no, my dear; I’m really a very good man, but I’m a very bad Wizard,

689
00:37:47,124 --> 00:37:48,324
I must admit.

690
00:37:48,624 --> 00:37:50,290
Can’t you give me brains?

691
00:37:50,590 --> 00:37:51,923
You don’t need them.

692
00:37:52,223 --> 00:37:54,689
You are learning something every day.

693
00:37:54,989 --> 00:37:57,922
A baby has brains, but it doesn’t know much.

694
00:37:58,222 --> 00:38:01,622
Experience is the only thing that brings knowledge,

695
00:38:01,922 --> 00:38:06,722
and the longer you are on earth the more experience you are sure to get.

696
00:38:07,022 --> 00:38:10,622
but I shall be very unhappy unless you give me brains.

697
00:38:10,922 --> 00:38:16,455
I’m not much of a magician, as I said; but if you will come to me tomorrow morning,

698
00:38:16,755 --> 00:38:19,088
I will stuff your head with brains.

699
00:38:19,388 --> 00:38:24,721
I cannot tell you how to use them, however; you must find that out for yourself.

700
00:38:25,021 --> 00:38:26,621
Oh, thank you—thank you!

701
00:38:26,921 --> 00:38:29,587
I’ll find a way to use them, never fear!

702
00:38:29,887 --> 00:38:31,553
But how about my courage?

703
00:38:31,853 --> 00:38:34,453
All you need is confidence in yourself.

704
00:38:34,753 --> 00:38:39,086
There is no living thing that is not afraid when it faces danger.

705
00:38:39,386 --> 00:38:43,186
The True courage is in facing danger when you are afraid,

706
00:38:43,486 --> 00:38:46,419
and that kind of courage you have in plenty.

707
00:38:46,719 --> 00:38:53,719
I shall really be very unhappy unless you give me the sort of courage that makes one forget he is afraid.

708
00:38:54,019 --> 00:38:55,285
How about my heart?

709
00:38:55,585 --> 00:38:58,118
I think you are wrong to want a heart.

710
00:38:58,418 --> 00:39:00,351
It makes most people unhappy.

711
00:39:00,651 --> 00:39:04,451
If you only knew it, you are in luck not to have a heart.

712
00:39:04,751 --> 00:39:08,884
For my part, I will bear all the unhappiness without a murmur,

713
00:39:09,184 --> 00:39:11,184
if you will give me the heart.

714
00:39:11,484 --> 00:39:14,617
Come to me tomorrow and you shall have a heart.

715
00:39:14,917 --> 00:39:21,050
I have played Wizard for so many years that I may as well continue the part a little longer.

716
00:39:21,350 --> 00:39:23,416
how am I to get back to Kansas?

717
00:39:23,716 --> 00:39:30,716
Give me two or three days to consider the matter and I’ll try to find a way to carry you over the desert.

718
00:39:31,016 --> 00:39:34,616
In the meantime you shall all be treated as my guests,

719
00:39:34,916 --> 00:39:40,982
and while you live in the Palace my people will wait upon you and obey your slightest wish.

720
00:39:41,282 --> 00:39:45,682
There is only one thing I ask in return for my help—such as it is.

721
00:39:45,982 --> 00:39:49,582
You must keep my secret and tell no one I am a humbug.

722
00:39:49,882 --> 00:39:51,082
Congratulate me.

723
00:39:51,382 --> 00:39:54,182
I am going to Oz to get my brains at last.

724
00:39:54,482 --> 00:39:57,282
When I return I shall be as other men are.

725
00:39:57,582 --> 00:40:04,715
But surely you will think more of me when you hear the splendid thoughts my new brain is going to turn out.

726
00:40:05,015 --> 00:40:07,948
You must excuse me for taking your head off,

727
00:40:08,248 --> 00:40:13,314
but I shall have to do it in order to put your brains in their proper place.

728
00:40:13,614 --> 00:40:16,414
You are quite welcome to take my head off,

729
00:40:16,714 --> 00:40:20,714
as long as it will be a better one when you put it on again.

730
00:40:21,014 --> 00:40:26,414
Hereafter you will be a great man, for I have given you a lot of bran-new brains.

731
00:40:26,714 --> 00:40:27,914
How do you feel?

732
00:40:28,214 --> 00:40:31,747
When I get used to my brains I shall know everything.

733
00:40:32,047 --> 00:40:35,847
Why are those needles and pins sticking out of your head?

734
00:40:36,147 --> 00:40:37,813
I have come for my heart.

735
00:40:38,113 --> 00:40:41,179
But I shall have to cut a hole in your breast,

736
00:40:41,479 --> 00:40:44,345
so I can put your heart in the right place.

737
00:40:44,645 --> 00:40:46,311
I hope it won’t hurt you.

738
00:40:46,611 --> 00:40:48,411
I shall not feel it at all.

739
00:40:48,711 --> 00:40:49,911
Isn’t it a beauty?

740
00:40:50,211 --> 00:40:51,411
It is, indeed!

741
00:40:51,711 --> 00:40:53,244
But is it a kind heart?

742
00:40:53,544 --> 00:40:57,010
now you have a heart that any man might be proud of.

743
00:40:57,310 --> 00:41:02,643
I’m sorry I had to put a patch on your breast, but it really couldn’t be helped.

744
00:41:02,943 --> 00:41:07,209
I am very grateful to you, and shall never forget your kindness.

745
00:41:07,509 --> 00:41:08,975
I will get it for you.

746
00:41:09,275 --> 00:41:10,475
What is it?

747
00:41:10,775 --> 00:41:13,841
if it were inside of you, it would be courage.

748
00:41:14,141 --> 00:41:17,807
You know, of course, that courage is always inside one;

749
00:41:18,107 --> 00:41:22,973
so that this really cannot be called courage until you have swallowed it.

750
00:41:23,273 --> 00:41:26,939
Therefore I advise you to drink it as soon as possible.

751
00:41:27,239 --> 00:41:28,572
How do you feel now?

752
00:41:28,872 --> 00:41:33,872
when all these people make me do things that everybody knows can’t be done?

753
00:41:34,172 --> 00:41:38,772
It was easy to make the Scarecrow and the Lion and the Woodman happy,

754
00:41:39,072 --> 00:41:41,872
because they imagined I could do anything.

755
00:41:42,172 --> 00:41:46,905
But it will take more than imagination to carry Dorothy back to Kansas,

756
00:41:47,205 --> 00:41:50,205
and I’m sure I don’t know how it can be done.

757
00:41:50,505 --> 00:41:55,771
Sit down, my dear; I think I have found the way to get you out of this country.

758
00:41:56,071 --> 00:41:57,337
And back to Kansas?

759
00:41:57,637 --> 00:42:01,103
for I haven’t the faintest notion which way it lies.

760
00:42:01,403 --> 00:42:04,669
But the first thing to do is to cross the desert,

761
00:42:04,969 --> 00:42:08,235
and then it should be easy to find your way home.

762
00:42:08,535 --> 00:42:10,335
How can I cross the desert?

763
00:42:10,635 --> 00:42:14,435
You see, when I came to this country it was in a balloon.

764
00:42:14,735 --> 00:42:18,601
You also came through the air, being carried by a cyclone.

765
00:42:18,901 --> 00:42:23,901
So I believe the best way to get across the desert will be through the air.

766
00:42:24,201 --> 00:42:27,667
Now, it is quite beyond my powers to make a cyclone;

767
00:42:27,967 --> 00:42:32,967
but I’ve been thinking the matter over, and I believe I can make a balloon.

768
00:42:33,267 --> 00:42:37,600
is made of silk, which is coated with glue to keep the gas in it.

769
00:42:37,900 --> 00:42:43,366
I have plenty of silk in the Palace, so it will be no trouble to make the balloon.

770
00:42:43,666 --> 00:42:49,199
But in all this country there is no gas to fill the balloon with, to make it float.

771
00:42:49,499 --> 00:42:51,299
it will be of no use to us.

772
00:42:51,599 --> 00:42:56,665
But there is another way to make it float, which is to fill it with hot air.

773
00:42:56,965 --> 00:42:58,898
Hot air isn’t as good as gas,

774
00:42:59,198 --> 00:43:04,064
for if the air should get cold the balloon would come down in the desert,

775
00:43:04,364 --> 00:43:05,830
and we should be lost.

776
00:43:06,130 --> 00:43:07,596
Are you going with me?

777
00:43:07,896 --> 00:43:10,162
I am tired of being such a humbug.

778
00:43:10,462 --> 00:43:15,928
If I should go out of this Palace my people would soon discover I am not a Wizard,

779
00:43:16,228 --> 00:43:20,361
and then they would be vexed with me for having deceived them.

780
00:43:20,661 --> 00:43:25,394
So I have to stay shut up in these rooms all day, and it gets tiresome.

781
00:43:25,694 --> 00:43:30,227
I’d much rather go back to Kansas with you and be in a circus again.

782
00:43:30,527 --> 00:43:33,660
Now, if you will help me sew the silk together,

783
00:43:33,960 --> 00:43:36,426
we will begin to work on our balloon.

784
00:43:36,726 --> 00:43:39,126
I am now going away to make a visit.

785
00:43:39,426 --> 00:43:42,692
While I am gone the Scarecrow will rule over you.

786
00:43:42,992 --> 00:43:45,792
I command you to obey him as you would me.

787
00:43:46,092 --> 00:43:47,292
Come, Dorothy!

788
00:43:47,592 --> 00:43:50,192
Hurry up, or the balloon will fly away.

789
00:43:50,492 --> 00:43:51,692
Come back!

790
00:43:51,992 --> 00:43:53,192
I want to go, too!

791
00:43:53,492 --> 00:43:55,158
Oz was always our friend.

792
00:43:55,458 --> 00:43:59,524
When he was here he built for us this beautiful Emerald City,

793
00:43:59,824 --> 00:44:04,224
and now he is gone he has left the Wise Scarecrow to rule over us.

794
00:44:04,524 --> 00:44:10,524
Truly I should be ungrateful if I failed to mourn for the man who gave me my lovely heart.

795
00:44:10,824 --> 00:44:14,090
I should like to cry a little because Oz is gone,

796
00:44:14,390 --> 00:44:18,656
if you will kindly wipe away my tears, so that I shall not rust.

797
00:44:18,956 --> 00:44:23,889
there is not another city in all the world that is ruled by a stuffed man.

798
00:44:24,189 --> 00:44:29,722
for this Palace and the Emerald City belong to us, and we can do just as we please.

799
00:44:30,022 --> 00:44:35,422
When I remember that a short time ago I was up on a pole in a farmer’s cornfield,

800
00:44:35,722 --> 00:44:39,122
and that now I am the ruler of this beautiful City,
